# Numbers with SI units. A unit is written right after the number, in SI,
# with no space: `5kg`, `300cm`, `20°C`. A unit of more than one term is
# joined with `.` and takes `^` and a whole exponent, `kg.m^2.s^-2`, and
# there is no `/`. The number is any number a Scientific is, so
# `10.5(3)e-3kg` has its uncertainty, and it propagates through arithmetic
# as it does for a Scientific.
#
# A sum and a comparison convert the right side to the unit of the left; a
# product and a quotient combine the units; a result with no dimension is a
# plain number. Two quantities of different dimensions are an error.
#
# A prefix and a `.` on their own is a scale: `1k.` is 1000, `5m.` is 0.005,
# and the number it makes is exact. `m` alone is the metre and `m.` is milli.

sheet Literals

| Name         | Value        |
| ------------ | ------------ |
| mass         | 5kg          |
| length       | 300cm        |
| acceleration | 9.81m.s^-2   |
| energy       | 5kg.m^2.s^-2 |
| force        | 2kN          |
| temperature  | 20°C         |
| uncertain    | 10.5(3)e-3kg |
| kilo         | 1.5k.        |
| milli        | 5m.          |
| micro        | 10uF         |
| duration     | 42s          |

sheet Arithmetic

| Name          | Value                     |
| ------------- | ------------------------- |
| sum           | = 5kg + 300g              |
| other way     | = 300g + 5kg              |
| difference    | = 5kg - 300g              |
| product       | = 2m * 3m                 |
| quotient      | = 10m / 4s                |
| scaled        | = 5kg * 2                 |
| power         | = 2m ** 3                 |
| root          | = sqrt(9m ** 2)           |
| no dimension  | = 6m / 3cm                |
| in grams      | = 5kg.to('g')             |
| in kelvin     | = 20°C.to('K')            |
| warmer by     | = 20°C - 10°C             |
| equal         | = 100cm == 1m             |
| ordered       | = 5kg < 6000g             |
| uncertain sum | = 3.0(3)m + 4.0(4)m      |
| plus-minus    | = 5kg ± 100g              |
| kilo of a sum | = 1.5k. + 500             |

# A column can be of a quantity type, here `Mass`: a `Quantity` of that
# dimension. A cell in another unit of it is converted to the column's unit, a
# bare number is read in that unit, and a cell of another dimension is an
# error. A column with no declared type, or `Quantity`, holds whatever it is
# given.
sheet Pantry

Item : String
Amount : Mass

| Item  | Amount  |
| ----- | ------- |
| flour | 5kg     |
| sugar | 300g    |
| salt  | 5       |
| yeast | 7.5(2)g |

# The unit in brackets after the type is the unit the column is shown and held
# in, whichever the cells were written in: a bare number is read in it. After
# a `;` a number format as a Scientific takes may follow, `Mass[mg; .1f]`.
# The unit is said once, in the column's head, and its cells are numbers; a
# `v` before the conversion puts it down in each row, `Mass[g; v]`.
sheet Display

Item   : String
Kilos  : Mass
Grams  : Mass[g]
Rows   : Mass[g; v]
Metres : Length

| Item  | Kilos | Grams | Rows | Metres  |
| ----- | ----- | ----- | ---- | ------- |
| flour | 5kg   | 5kg   | 5kg  | 2m      |
| sugar | 300g  | 300g  | 300g | 150     |
| salt  | 5     | 5     | 5    | 3.5(2)m |

# With no unit before the `;`, each value is shown in its own unit. The
# conversion `s` lets the uncertainty lead: a value that has one is shown by
# it, and an exact value, which has none, in nine significant digits.
sheet Constants

Value : Quantity[; .9s]

| Name                | Value             |
| ------------------- | ----------------- |
| Newton              | 6.674_30(15)e-11m |
| reduced Planck      | 1.054571817e-34J.s |
| Faraday             | 96485.33212331C   |
| whole               | 5kg               |

sheet Totals

| Name     | Value                         |
| -------- | ----------------------------- |
| total    | = Pantry.Amount.sum()         |
| mean     | = Pantry.Amount.mean()        |
| largest  | = Pantry.Amount.max()         |
| smallest | = Pantry.Amount.min()         |
| in grams | = Pantry.Amount.sum().to('g') |

# A computed column of quantities may be declared too: it is held in the unit
# of its type, so the product of two lengths is an area in square metres
# whatever units the lengths were written in.
sheet Boxes

Width     : Length
Height    : Length
Area      : Area
Perimeter : Length

| Box   | Width | Height | Area | Perimeter |
| ----- | ----- | ------ | ---- | --------- |
| small | 20cm  | 30cm   | *    | *         |
| big   | 2m    | 150cm  | *    | *         |

Area      := Width * Height
Perimeter := 2 * (Width + Height)

# Data over an area, as a disk drive's: `DataDensity` holds `b.m^-2`, here
# shown in `Tib.m^-2`. A drive's capacity over its platter area gives it, and
# data units take the binary and SI prefixes alike (see `data.omx`).
sheet Drives

Drive    : String
Capacity : Data[GiB]
Platter  : Area[cm^2]
Density  : DataDensity[Tib.m^-2]

| Drive | Capacity | Platter | Density |
| ----- | -------- | ------- | ------- |
| old   | 500GiB   | 100cm^2  | *       |
| new   | 4TiB     | 100cm^2  | *       |
| tiny  | 128MiB   | 5cm^2   | *       |

Density := Capacity / Platter

# Data: data in bytes and bits, with the binary prefixes (`KiB` is 1024 B)
# and the SI ones (`kB` is 1000 B). A column of `Data` shows its cells in
# the unit it names, `Data[MiB]`, whichever they were written in, and a
# `DataRate[Mib.s^-1]` column holds mebibits a second. Dividing a size in
# mebibits by such a rate leaves seconds.
sheet Data

File     : String
Size     : Data[MiB]
Rate     : DataRate[Mib.s^-1]
Seconds  : Quantity

| File   | Size   | Rate        | Seconds |
| ------ | ------ | ----------- | ------- |
| photo  | 4MiB   | 100Mib.s^-1 | *       |
| film   | 1.5GiB | 50Mib.s^-1  | *       |
| backup | 8GiB   | 1Gib.s^-1   | *       |
| log    | 640KiB | 8Mib.s^-1   | *       |
| dump   | 1MiB   | 100Mib.s^-1 | *       |

Seconds := Size.to('Mib') / Rate

# Metric units outside the SI, which the SI accepts or science relies on:
# the litre (`l` or `L`), tonne `t` with `kt` and `Mt`, carat `ct`, hectare
# `ha` and `are`, `barn`, `bar`, `atm` and `torr`, electronvolt `eV`,
# calorie `cal` (4.184 J), watt-hour `Wh`, the metric horsepower `mhp`, dalton
# `Da`, the astronomical `au`, `ly`, `ld` and `pc`, `Å`, the nautical mile
# `nmi` and knot `kn`, the year `yr`, `gon` and `rev`, and `rem`, `Ci`, `R`,
# `Jy`, `tog` and `sverdrup`. They are exact multiples of SI units and most
# take the prefixes (`ml`, `kWh`, `keV`, `kcal`, `kpc`, `mCi`), except `t`,
# `ha` and the like. `u` is read as the prefix micro and shown as `μ`.
# Imperial and US units (`ft`, `lb`, `gal`, `psi`, `°F`) are units too, and no cgs ones. Each row is a
# quantity and the same amount in another unit.
sheet Metric

Item   : String
Amount : Quantity
Same   : Quantity

| Item     | Amount  | Same                 |
| -------- | ------- | -------------------- |
| tank     | 2.5L    | = (2.5L).to('dm^3')  |
| can      | 330ml   | = (330ml).to('L')    |
| cargo    | 1.2t    | = (1.2t).to('kg')    |
| field    | 3ha     | = (3ha).to('m^2')    |
| tyre     | 2.2bar  | = (2.2bar).to('kPa') |
| snack    | 250kcal | = (250kcal).to('kJ') |
| battery  | 12kWh   | = (12kWh).to('MJ')   |
| photon   | 511keV  | = (511keV).to('fJ')  |
| carbon   | 12Da    | = (12Da).to('yg')    |
| hair     | 70um    | = (70um).to('mm')    |
| star     | 4.25ly  | = (4.25ly).to('pc')  |
| orbit    | 1au     | = (1au).to('Gm')     |
| gem      | 5ct     | = (5ct).to('g')      |
| weather  | 1atm    | = (1atm).to('kPa')   |
| engine   | 100mhp  | = (100mhp).to('kW')   |
| ship     | 12kn    | = (12kn).to('km.h^-1') |
| lens     | 5Å      | = (5Å).to('nm')      |
| target   | 12barn  | = (12barn).to('fm^2') |
| scan     | 50rem   | = (50rem).to('mSv')  |
| source   | 3Ci     | = (3Ci).to('GBq')    |
| current  | 15sverdrup | = (15sverdrup).to('hm^3.s^-1') |

# Time is a kind of quantity too, `Duration`, held in seconds, `s`, with the
# units `min`, `h`, `d` and `wk`. A duration is written as a quantity,
# `90min`, in ISO 8601, `PT1H30M`, or with a sign and a colon, `+01:30` (an
# unsigned `01:30` is a time of day). A bare `Duration` is `Duration[s]`; the
# unit goes in the head, and `iso` or `%H:%M:%S` show the whole of it. A
# distance over a duration is a speed, and a speed times a duration a distance.
sheet Durations

Task    : String
Spent   : Duration[min]
Hours   : Duration[h; .2f]
Clock   : Duration[%H:%M:%S]
Iso     : Duration[iso]
Seconds : Duration
Share   : Float[.2f]
Pace    : Speed[km.h^-1; .1f]

| Task    | Spent    | Hours | Clock | Iso | Seconds | Share | Pace |
| ------- | -------- | ----- | ----- | --- | ------- | ----- | ---- |
| plan    | 45min    | *     | *     | *   | *       | *     | *    |
| build   | PT2H     | *     | *     | *   | *       | *     | *    |
| test    | +01:30   | *     | *     | *   | *       | *     | *    |
| review  | 2700s    | *     | *     | *   | *       | *     | *    |
| ship    | 0.1d     | *     | *     | *   | *       | *     | *    |

Hours   := Spent
Clock   := Spent
Iso     := Spent
Seconds := Spent
Share   := Spent / Durations.Spent.sum()
Pace    := 12km / Spent

# A duration may have an uncertainty, `10.5(3)s`, as any quantity does. A
# whole number of microseconds cannot hold one, so a value that has one is
# held as a quantity of seconds, and it passes through a sum or a product as
# an uncertain value does. It cannot move a date or a time, which are exact.
sheet Stopwatch

Lap    : String
Time   : Duration[s; ±.1f]
Total  : Duration[s; ±.1f]
Double : Duration[s; .1f]

| Lap    | Time     | Total | Double |
| ------ | -------- | ----- | ------ |
| first  | 10.5(3)s | *     | *      |
| second | 90(2)s   | *     | *      |
| third  | PT1M     | *     | *      |

Total  := Time + PT10S
Double := Time * 2

sheet Flight

